<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!-- The hardened runtime is required for notarization. These two exceptions
       are what a bundled CPython needs and nothing more.

       disable-library-validation: the interpreter loads extension modules
       (xxhash, numpy, Pillow) at runtime from inside the bundle. Without this
       the hardened runtime refuses them and the server dies on import.

       allow-dyld-environment-variables: PyInstaller's bootloader sets library
       search paths for the interpreter it just unpacked.

       Deliberately absent: allow-unsigned-executable-memory and allow-jit.
       Nothing here compiles code at runtime, and an exception that is not
       needed is an exception that should not be asked for.

       There is no app-sandbox entitlement. A sandboxed application cannot read
       an arbitrary camera card or write to an arbitrary drive, which is the
       entire job. Access is governed by the ordinary macOS files-and-folders
       prompts instead, described in Info.plist. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key><true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables</key><true/>
</dict>
</plist>
